  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "The cut at the Masters is a tournament rule that reduces the field to the top 50 players (plus ties) after 36 holes (two rounds). Players with scores higher than this threshold do not advance to the weekend rounds. The 2026 Masters cut was set at 4-over 148.Key Details About the CutCurrent Rule: Only the top 50 and ties make the cut.10-Shot Rule Removed: Previously, players within 10 strokes of the lead also made the cut, but this rule was removed in 2020 to make the field smaller and more competitive.Difficulty: This is the most challenging cut among all four men's major golf championships.When it Happens: The cut is finalized after the completion of the second round on Friday.Usage Examples & Context\"Making the Cut\": Players who finish in the top 50 (or ties) after Friday continue playing Saturday and Sunday, ensuring them a share of the prize money.\"Missing the Cut\": Players who fall outside the top 50 (e.g., scoring +5 or higher when the cut is +4) are eliminated.\"The Bubble\": Players sitting right on the score line (e.g., 4-over in 2026) are considered to be \"on the bubble,\" as their status can change with each hole.Synonyms/Related TermsCut line: The specific score to par required to advance.Weekend play: The final two rounds (3 and 4).Weekend warrior: Often used to describe a player who safely made the cut.Historically, the cut at the Masters usually hovers between 2-over and 6-over, with a record low of even-par (2020) and a record high of 10-over (1982).",
